            0.148
          ],
          "content": "by explicitly performing the long divisions and expressing these rational numbers as decimals."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.155,
            0.886,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "2. Perform the long division for $\\frac { 1 } { 1 3 }$ . Identify the repeating block of digits. Does it show cyclic properties if you evaluate $\\frac { 2 } { 1 3 } ?$ Now compute $\\frac{3}{13},   \\frac{4}{13},$ , etc. What do you notice?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.246,
            0.776,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "3. Classify the following numbers as rational or irrational:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.275,
            0.283,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "(i) $\\sqrt { 8 1 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.308,
            0.283,
            0.331
          ],
          "content": "(ii) $\\sqrt { 1 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.336,
            0.344,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "(iii) 0.33333 …"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.364,
            0.469,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": "(iv) 0.123451234512345 …"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.391,
            0.884,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": "(v) 1.01001000100001 … (Notice the pattern: Is it repeating a single block?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.438,
            0.531,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": "(vi) 23.560185612239874790120"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.466,
            0.723,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "Find the explicit fractions in case they are rational."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.494,
            0.885,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "4. The number 0.9 (which means 0.99999 … ) is a rational number. Using algebra (let $\\underline{x} \\cong 0.\\overline{9},$ multiply by 10, and subtract), explain why 0.9 is exactly equal to 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.57,
            0.885,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "\\*5. We have seen that the repeating block of $\\frac { 1 } { 7 }$ is a cyclic number. Try to find more numbers (n) whose reciprocals $\\left( { \\frac { 1 } { n } } \\right)$ produce decimals with repeating blocks that are cyclic."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.657,
            0.885,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": "**Non-uniqueness of decimal representations.** Just as $1 = { \\frac { 1 0 } { 1 0 } } = { \\frac { 1 0 0 } { 1 0 0 } } ,$ rational numbers can have two decimal forms. Any terminating decimal has an alternative with repeating 9s: 1.000 … = 0.999 …, 2.47000 … = 2.46999 …."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.754,
            0.886,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "Is it not surprising that 0.999 … = 1? Many would have guessed that it is slightly less than 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.818,
            0.728,
            0.843
          ],
          "level": 2,
